  "analysis": "We have a single problem instance: a connected undirected graph with n vertices, m edges, minimum degree at least 3, and an integer k (1 ≤ k ≤ n). The task is to either output a simple path of length at least ceil(n/k), or output k simple cycles (each of length at least 3 and not divisible by 3) with a representative vertex that belongs only to that cycle among all printed cycles. If neither is possible, output -1. The common constructive approach is to perform a DFS and try to either (a) find a path from the root to a node with depth >= ceil(n/k) - 1, or (b) collect back-edges to ancestors to form cycles. If during DFS we can find such a back-edge (v, a) that creates a cycle of length depth[v] - depth[a] + 1 not divisible by 3, we can emit a cycle with representative v. We iterate through back-edges; each vertex is used as a representative at most once. If we exhaust the DFS without obtaining a long path or k valid cycles, the problem may be unsatisfiable and we print -1. The implementation will use an iterative DFS to avoid recursion depth issues for n up to 2.5e5, track depth and parent, and generate cycles on-the-fly. If we find a path early, we print PATH immediately; otherwise, after collecting up to k cycles, we print CYCLES; if not enough, print -1. This approach runs in O(n + m) time with output bounded by 1e6 numbers as guaranteed by the problem.\n",
  "plan": "Plan to implement a C++ solution that:\n- Reads n, m, k and the graph.\n- Computes L = ceil(n/k).\n- Performs an iterative DFS from node 1, tracking depth and parent.\n- While visiting a new node, if depth >= L-1, immediately print PATH with the path from root to that node and exit.\n- For every back-edge (v, a) to an ancestor a (depth[a] < depth[v]), attempt to form a cycle using v as the representative: cycle length len = depth[v] - depth[a] + 1. If len % 3 != 0 and v has not yet been used as a representative, record the cycle (v, a), mark v as used, and if we collected k cycles, print CYCLES by listing for each cycle the vertices along the tree path from v up to a (in order: v, parent[v], ..., a).\n- If DFS ends with fewer than k cycles and no long path found, print -1.\n- Ensure not to exceed the 1e6 total output numbers by relying on the problem's guarantee.\nThe code will be self-contained in main.cpp and compiled with C++17.\n",
